To install the system: 

        - Make sure you have 6 MB free space on the disc on which you
          are going to install the system.

        - Run the INSTALL program on the installation disk.


To turn your PC into an RC4000:

        - make the install directory (the directory that is current when
          the install program terminates) current, and run RC.BAT,
          which is placed there.

        - At the "Initialise date:" prompt, answer Alt-D (or type the
          date and time yourself). 

        You are up and running!


To turn your PC back into a PC:

        - Press SysRq (normally Alt-PrintScreen).

        - Press Alt-X.


A little warning:
        If you compile an algol program in an all-process, the file holding
        the RC4000 disc image will expand to its maximum size (more than
        4 MB). To avoid this, always set your process disc resources close
        to what is actually needed when compiling.

There are NO help facilities available in the emulator (nor in the
installation program). So don't look for them!

If you got the RC4000 emulator up and running, and wonder what to do then,
take a look in the file HINTS.TXT.
*****************************************************************************


System requirements
*******************

Processor: 80386 or higher. NDP (80387 or the like) required.

Memory   :
           Conventional: Appr. 300KB, dep. on actual configuration.

           Extended    : Appr. 1 MB (preferably more), under control of a
                         VCPI-compliant EMS emulator (EMM386, QEMM or the
                         like).

Disc     : Min. 5MB available space on hard disc recommended.

Disc Cache:
           SMARTDRV Write-Behind caching is strongly recommended.

DOS Environment:
           Appr. 50 free bytes needed if RC.BAT is used to start the
           emulator.

                  
Preferably, remove all resident software not needed to run the emulator
(pop-up programs etc). Networking SW may also interfere with the emulator
in an undesired way.


Limitations
***********

The protection facilities of RC4000 are not implemented in the emulator
(Monitor/Task mode switching, Protection Register, Protection Keys).

Low precision floating point mode is not implemented.

The emulator is not able to perform disc I/O concurrent with program 
execution. As a consequence, the "CPU time" used by a process according
to the process description will also include disc I/O time. So if you want
to compare CPU performance to a physical RC4000/RC8000 from the CPU time,
use jobs with little disc activity.

The above limitation does not cause any other practical problems. Nor has
it caused need for any change in the original disc driver code in the
RC4000 monitor. If disc caching is used as suggested, the disc system of
the emulator can outperform any RC4000/RC8000 anyway.

If any physical disc operation or cached disc operation performed by DOS
takes more than 25.6ms to complete, there is a danger of loosing timer
interrupts in RC4000. This means that the date-and-time clock maintained by
the monitor will be too slow during heavy load. Apart from this, no
serious consequences of this problem are known.


Installation overview
*********************

The following files are installed:
    
    In the base directory specified to the install program:

        RC.BAT          Batch file to start the RC4000 emulator.

        READ.ME         This file.

        HINTS.TXT       Getting Started guide


    In the CTRL subdirectory:

        RC4000.EXE      The emulator program.

        RC4000.CFG      Default configuration file, specifying the
                        hardware configuration of the emulated RC4000.

        MONITOR         Binary RC4000 monitor.


    In the DRIVES subdirectory:

        UNIT0           Image of an RC4000 system disc, containing the
                        basic software: monitor, fp and utilities. algol 
                        compiler and various other files. Also, bprimo, btem 
                        and bsos (part of the MIPS/TS system) are found. 

        MKDISC.EXE      Program to create an empty RC4819 disc image file.
                        Use: MKDISC UNIT1 to create a file called UNIT1 to
                        be used as RC4819 image. The file must not exist
                        already.



Configuration file
******************

The emulator gets information on the wanted RC4000 hardware configuration
from a configuration file. The configuration file is pointed out by
the DOS environment variable RC4000CFG. If this variable is not found, 
the default configuration file name is RC4000.CFG. The configuration file
is first searched for in the DOS current directory; then in the directory
where the RC4000.EXE file is located.

The configuration file defines the min. and max. size of Core Store,
specified as no. of RC4000 halfwords. The Core Store allocated will
be as large as possible, not exceeding the Max. specification. The size
is always cut to an integer multiple of 2048 halfwords. If it is not
possible to allocate at least Min. halfwords, the emulator will terminate
immediately with an error message.

If you want the largest possible core store, specify 8388605 for Max.
However, at present the size is limited to 6MHW - but sizes above 1MHW are
probably not very usefull, considering that RC4000 does not support
process relocation, as RC8000 does.

The RC4000.CFG file supplied on the installation disc contains a list
of all possible device kinds and the syntax for specifying them. Here 
follows some additional notes on each supported device kind.

                        
READER          The "paper tape" is read from a DOS file. Yoy will probably 
                only use the paper tape reader in case you want to try out
                the original way of autoloading RC4000 (as per default, the
                emulator performs the autoload directly from a DOS file).
                For this purpose, it is convenient to install the paper tape
                by setting a DOS environment variable, RC2000, to point to
                the file holding the autoload tape image.
                See RC.BAT for an example.
                Paper tape can also be installed by pressing Alt-C when
                the emulator is in Control Panel mode.

PUNCH           Only needed to produce autoload tapes. The paper tape
                image goes to a DOS file with the name given in the DOS
                environment varable RC150. This means 
                that you only can produce one paper tape per emulation
                session. Then you must rename the file by means of DOS.
                If RC150 is undefined, the punch will return an error status.

CONSOLE         A terminal complying to the TTY console interface, 
                emulated on the CRT and keyboard of the PC. If more 
                CONSOLE's and/or RC851's are defined, they are implicitly 
                numbered from 1 and up after the sequence in which they
                appear in the configuration file. No. 1 is in foreground
                initially; switching between consoles is done by pressing
                Alt-digit, i.e. Alt-2 to switch to console no. 2. etc.
                There is no way to identify which console is in foreground.
                CONSOLE devices has very limited control character handling,
                but the advantage over RC851's is that CONSOLE's display 
                the number of lines and characters on the screen that
                were set when the emulator was called up, i.e. 50 line
                (and more) display is possible.
                For all consoles/terminals, the "key" interrupt is generated
                by pressing the Esc key when the device is not in input mode.
                (Esc also gives att when pressed during input, but that
                is due to the RC4000 monitor).
                
RC851           Similar to CONSOLE, but handles RC822 control sequences
                (=RC851 without Page Mode / Protected Fields). Always
                displays 24 lines on screen.

COMTERM         Terminal connection on COM port. Looks as a CONSOLE from
                inside RC4000, i.e. no TMX-like interrupt expansion.
                No kind of flow control or connect/disconnect control
                is implmented at present.

CLOCK           Only one of these should be defined, having device no. 3.
                Normally, the 100us resolution found on RC4000/RC8000 is
                emulated quite precisely. However some IBM PS/2 models are
                not quite IBM compatible(!) in a respect that makes it
                virtually impossible to emulate a resolution better than
                one timeslice (25.6 ms). You can tell if your computer
                is one of these from the start-up display of the emulator.
                Clock resolution is here stated to either 100us or 25.6ms.

DFC403          One DFC403 disc controller supports up to 6 RC4819 discs.
                You may include more DF403's in the configuration (but
                you will need to build your own monitor, then).

RC4819          The disc image is kept in a DOS file, the name of which is
                stated in the configuration file. In case you dont't 
                know: RC4819 capacity is 9 segment/track * 20 tracks/cylinder
                * 406 cylinders = 73080 segments. However, it is strongly
                recommended to keep the actual disc size lower than this,
                by using a lower slice size than the standard 36 segments.
                Use more discs instead, if you need more capacity, assuming
                you don't need to store very large single files.
                The reasons for this suggestion is, among other things,
                that DOS performance on direct access to a file degrades
                severely with increasing file size. You may find that
                the time needed for e.g. an algol compilation varies
                quite much, depending on where the object file is placed
                on the (virtual) disc: Lower slice numbers means faster
                compilation!

                Especially, it may be a good idea to keep the system disc
                small, mainly because it is more complicated to reorganise
                the system disc than a non-system disc.

                Disc image files are created by the MKDISC program placed 
                in the DRIVES subdirectory, see Installation Overview above.
                Files are created small and dynamically extended, so the DOS
                file size reflects the highest cylinder that were ever
                written to on the disc. Reading segments that has not been
                written results in no error but returns undefined data.

                Note that the algol compiler always extends its output file
                to appr. the maximum possible size within the BS claims of
                the internal process, and also during compilation writes 
                intermediate data to the file backwards, starting at the file
                end. So, if you compile an algol program (however small) 
                in an all-process, the disc is expanded to its maximum size.
                So don't do that, if you don't want that to happen!

                If disc operations terminate with status "parity error",
                the reason is probably that the system is out of DOS disc
                space to extend the disc image file.

RC4819RAM       A RC4819 disc where data is kept in extended memory
                (allocated through EMS/VCPI) rather than in a DOS file.
                The performance gain is mainly obtained by not having to
                go through the DOS file system, rather than by avoiding
                physical disc accesses, assuming you use SMARTDRV as
                suggested. A RC4819RAM disc is useful for holding the
                object file of large algol compilations, if you want
                ultimate performance.

                The disc size is stated in no. of cylinders in the
                configuration file (one cylinder = 180 segments
                = 138240 bytes). RAM discs are allocated in memory before
                allocation of the Core Store, so you may still specify 
                maximum value for core store size.

NOTE: THE RC4819 DISCS AND THE DFC403 CONTROLLER ARE NOT USED IN THE
      DISTRIBUTION VERSION OF THE RC4000 EMULATOR ANYMORE. INSTEAD THE
      FOLLOWING DEVICE KIND IS USED:

RC433X          This is an alternative virtual disk, specially tailored for
                the RC4000 emulator. It gives better performance than the
                hardware emulation of DFC403/RC4819. RC433X disks uses no
                interrupt channels on RC4000. The (virtual) disk format is
                the same as for RC4819: 180 segm/cyl. etc. 
                In the monitor, the RC433 driver has been modified to deal 
                with this special disk device. 
                Most of the comments on RC4819 above goes for RC433X as well.

RC433XRAM       RAM version of RC433X. Size is specified as for RC4919RAM.

DOS             This is a virtual device providing access to the DOS file
                system. The supplied monitor for RC4000 includes a driver
                for the device; no further documentation is given here.


Program call parameters:
************************

The following parameters to RC4000.EXE are possible (see also RC.BAT for
an example):

/A    Autoload from paper tape on startup. Requires the RC2000 DOS environment
      variable to point to a DOS file with a paper tape image.

/A:<file>
      Autoload and start the binary monitor stored in the DOS file with the 
      name stated. This is the way autoload is done in the distribution
      version of the emulator system.

/S    Only meaningfull together with /A:<file>. Sets the "autostart" flag
      in the monitor, causing (1) the system disk to be mounted,
      (2) further s commands to be read automatically from the file 
      "startarea".
      Note that only the system disk is mounted. This also goes for the
      oldcat command of s in general. Mount further disks by placing
      "kit" commands in "startarea".

/:    ...if you don't want to see my name every time you start the emulator.
      (Pressing any key will also remove the start-up screen).

Parameters must be sepearated by a blank.

                
Problems during Start up
************************

Error messages are intended to be self-explanatory. The most likely problems
you will see is lack of memory and errors in the configuration file (in case
you modified it).

There are several different messages indicating lack of memory resources.
In general, if the message includes the word "heap" the problem is in
conventional memory, otherwise in extended/EMS/VCPI memory.

One special error message should be mentioned here:

        VCPI server not found.

This means that your EMS emulator (in case you installed one at all!) does
not support VCPI (Virtual Control Program Interface), OR that you attempted
to start the RC4000 emulator under MS-Windows or a similar system. That
is not possible.

EMM386 found in MS-DOS version 5 and 6 supports VCPI. So does QEMM-386;
the emulator was tested with version 5.11 and 6.02.

In case your systems hangs during start up of the emulator: Try to decrease
the max. core size to somewhat below what should be physically possible.
Especially if you are using DOS 5.0/EMM386, as this version of EMM386 
seems to have a bug in its VCPI memory accounting logic.


Monitor notes
*************

The installed system disc is configured with a slice length og 3 segments,
2030 slices, and subcat size = 36 segments, maincat size = 72 segments.
The maincat size may be changed with the s-command maincat, after autoloading
without "automatic startup".

The monitor is stored in the DOS file MONITOR in the CTRL subdirectory and
is loaded by the emulator.

The monitor includes the following devices:

Devno.  Devname   Interrupt        Driver kind

  0     reader        9             RC2000
  1     punch        12             RC150
  2     console1  Io:6 Key:3        RC315/modified for TTY
  3     clock        11             Interval timer
  4     console2  Io:20 Key:19      RC315/modified for TTY
  5     terminal1 Io:16 Key:15      RC315/modified for TTY
  6     terminal2 Io:14 Key:13      RC315/modified for TTY
 15     disc          -             RC433X disc drive
 16     disc1         -             RC433X disc drive
 17     disc2         -             RC433X disc drive
 18     disc3         -             RC433X disc drive
 19     disc4         -             RC433X disc drive
 20     disc5         -             RC433X disc drive
 21     disc6         -             RC433X disc drive
 22     disc7         -             RC433X disc drive
 63     dos          21             Virtual DOS interface device


The RC4000 monitor does not support logical discs (i.e. logical discs =
physical discs). But the format of chainhead etc. is compatible with
RC8000 Basic System Version 1, i.e. the utility programs disccopy,
kiton/kitoff/kitlabel etc. can be used.

Max. no. of slices on a disc is 2030.

The monitor supports most RC8000 Basic System Version 1 calls, including
General Copy (monitor procedure 84). Also, the "Message Identification Flag"
in message buffers is supported (used e.g. in connection with the algol
"activity" concept). The format of some internal structures are different,
mainly due to the different protection concepts in RC4000 and RC8000.


s notes
*******

The s operating system included supports catalog handling commands (kit,
nokit, kitlabel, maincat, ...). The code for these commands are removed
when the first internal process is created.

In some cases, the catalog handling commands reports errors for no
obvious reasons. If you are affected by this, use the corresponding
utility programs instead if possible.

The "oldcat" action of s has been changed to only mount the system disc,
not all discs defined. This is done to facilitate the use of RAM discs
which must be kitlabel'ed before mounting. When using "automatic start up",
place the proper s commands for mounting non-system discs in the startarea
file.


Interface to DOS file system
****************************

Transfering files between RC4000 and the DOS file system is done by means
of two RC4000 utility programs, fromdos and todos. These programs interact
with a special external device in the monitor with the devicename dos 
(deviceno. 63 in the supplied monitor). The driver for this device 
interacts with a virtual I/O device in the emulator. Neither of these
interfaces are documented here, but the utility programs are:


        fromdos
        *******

        Call:
            <file>=fromdos [conv.bin] <dosfile>

        Copies a DOS file into RC4000. <file> is the name of the RC4000
        destination file, <dosfile> is the name of the DOS file. 
        <dosfile> may be given as a "general text parameter" starting
        with ", and as such include a normal DOS path string. Use ù for
        backslash (made either by the ù key or by the backslash key).

        Without the conv.bin parameter, fromdos converts from DOS text
        file conventions to RC4000 conventions. With conv.bin, binary
        files can be copied; this only makes sense when transfering files
        between different RC4000/RC8000 systems (emulated or the real ones).
        The catalog tail cannot be transfered this way, but you may
        transfer a binout file that can hold full catalog entries.


        todos
        *****

        Call:

            <dosfile>=todos [conv.bin] <file>
                or
            todos [conv.bin] to.<dosfile> <file>

        The second form makes it possible to use DOS file names with
        extension, or including path components.

        The function of todos is similar to fromdos.

        Both programs writes a line on current output, stating no. of bytes
        transfered. The number is NOT equal to the character count given
        by the RC4000 copy utility.

        Note: Both todos and fromdos accept the parameter "conv.iso", which
        works as non-bin mode, but without 7-to-8 bit conversion of danish
        caracters ae oe aa etc. In certain cases, this may be usefull in
        connection with todos.

        WARNING: Using fromdos and todos directly on floppy disk files 
        is strongly discouraged.


More about disks and autoload:
******************************

The distribution RC4000 emulator is configured to use the emulator-specific
RC433X discs. If you got a previous version running with RC4819 disks, just
keep your old disk image file(s) and "paper tape" files and use them with
the new RC4000.EXE.

When using the RC433X disks, the only way to autoload the monitor into RC4000
is to let the emulator do it from a DOS file (the /A: parameter). This is
because the paper tape based autoloader interfaces directly to the DFC403
controller hardware, which is different from the RC433X.

If you find it blasphemic to use the emulator-specific disks and just want
the "real thing", here's an outline of how to do it:

- Inside RC4000, perform "stdmon=todos conv.bin stdmonitor"

- Leave the emulator. Go to the CTRL subdirectory and edit RC4000.CFG:
  Enable DFC403/RC4819 lines and disable RC433X by moving semicolons.

- Enter DOS commands:
      SET RC150=STDAUTO.AUT
      RC4000 /A:STDMON /S
  At this point, you may receive a message from the emulator about correction
  of an illegal file size. This can be ignored.

- Now in RC4000, from an all-process enter:
  autoloader stdmonitor

- Leave emulator. Enter the DOS command:
      SET RC150=

- Edit the file RC.BAT in the "install root" directory (C:\RC4000). Add:
  @SET RC2000=C:\RC4000\CTRL\STDAUTO.AUT
  ... and change the call of the emulator to:
  @RC4000 /A

- Calling RC.BAT, RC4000 will now prompt you: devi/disc=15/0?
  As real RC4000 users know, the proper answer to this is a Line Feed 
  (ctrl-J). Hopefully, the monitor is now loaded and autostart is 
  performed.

It may be wise to make an additional autoload tape without "autostart". Just
use the program manuloader instead of autoloader to do this.

Remember that the file stdmonitor must not be moved from its absolute
position on the disc. In case of emergency, use the DOS copy of the monitor
you made (stdmon) to recover.


A note about Algol:
*******************

The procedures system, monitor, get/set/share/zone(6) originally did not
support fielding of their array parameters: The arrays were always accessed
from their lowest index and onwards. This was changed by RC at a late
time, causing severe compatibility problems with existing code.

The Algol system on the emulator system disk is tailored to use the "old"
versions of the procedures mentioned. The "new" versions are available under
names prefixed with an "n" (nmonitor, nsystem etc.). If you want the "new"
procedures as default, this is possible through simple renaming.

The Algol version is, from what I know, the latest version from RC9000-10
Compiler Collection.

It may be of academic interest to note that the newer versions of Algol 
actually will run on a "real" RC4000 only if a small patch is made in the 
runtime procedure "tofrom" (in the GG instruction testing the CPU version).
By a kind of coincidence, this patch is not needed on the emulator.


A note about MIPS/TS:
*********************

You will find ready-to-use bsos, btem and bprimo files on the system disc.
Also, there are s command files named startsos, starttem, startprimo and
startmips. You will probably need to modify these (epspecially startsos) 
with respect to disk resources.

There is no SOS user catalog (soscat), however. You have to either copy
a soscat from another RC4000/8000/9000, or make one using upsoscat.

It's not the latest release of MIPS/TS, but it works! Don't think of running
TAS on the emulator; for several reasons TAS does not run on RC4000.


Other SW packages:
******************

System Utility is a RC8000 version from 1987. You can probably replace it 
with a newer version if you like.

The Screen Editor will not run on the emulator for the time being, due to
missing facilities in the RC4000 Terminal Process.

BOSS has never been tried out on the emulator. Maybe the missing protection
system will cause problems.

Other compilers than Algol (and Slang) hasn't been tried out. It is however
known that the Pascal system will run on RC4000 if a patch is made to the
exception handler of the Pascal Runtime Library.


Control Panel mode
******************

When you press SysRq during emulation, of if you start RC4000.EXE without the
/a option, you enter Control Panel mode. Here you can inspect and modify 
core store and the RC4000 registers.

The most essential functions keys in Control Panel mode:

F9      Resume emulation.

Alt-A   Autoload (RC4000 Autoload key action).

Alt-R   Reset (RC4000 Reset key action).

Alt-S   Start (RC4000 Start key action).

Alt-C   Install paper tape in reader.

Alt-X   Return to DOS.

Use of inspect/modify/trace facilities will not be documented for now. A
qualified hacker should not be stopped by this, I think!


